$video_thumbnailer — contact-sheet preview from video

  Builds one JPEG per videos[] entry: a grid of frames sampled across the
  timeline, with optional metadata header (file size, codec, duration, etc.).

Syntax:
  $video_thumbnailer
  $video_thumbnailer(width=1280, columns=4, rows=4, skip_seconds=5)
  $video_thumbnailer(no_header=1, comment_text='draft cut')

Input:
  videos[] — required; one preview sheet per video file

Output:
  images[] — one .jpg per input video (videos[] cleared)

Layout (defaults match pyvideothumbnailer-style tools):
  width=1024          — sheet width in px
  columns=5, rows=5   — thumbnail grid
  vertical_video_columns=, vertical_video_rows= — override grid for portrait video
  spacing=2           — px gap between cells and border
  background_color=black
  skip_seconds=10     — skip this many seconds before first frame

Header / labels:
  no_header=0|1
  header_font=, header_font_size=14, header_font_color=white
  timestamp_font=, timestamp_font_size=12
  timestamp_font_color=white
  timestamp_shadow_color=black|none
  comment_label=Comment:, comment_text=

Output quality:
  jpeg_quality=95

Deps (subprocess installs via uv run --extra video_thumbnailer):
  uv sync --extra video_thumbnailer   (PyAV, pymediainfo, Pillow)
  If you use .venvs/media: re-run tools/setup_external_venvs.ps1

Emulation:
  AH_EMULATE_VIDEO_THUMBNAILER=1

Example:
  @preview: $file('clip.mp4') -> $video_thumbnailer -> $save('clip_sheet.jpg')

Prompt-consuming: No — prompts[] pass through unchanged.
